WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.020
In about ninety seconds, we’ll set up an
agent with a Submilli

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:08.180
runtime that allows it to process a
customer's billing inquiry,

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:11.540
without giving it access to any data it
doesn't need in

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:13.380
order to resolve the ticket.

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:16.660
We will create a local "billing package"
that will expose

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:19.860
the billing services, and serve it to the
agent under a

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:21.620
blueprint you approve.

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:25.460
The host application will provide the
customer ID in question,

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:28.740
and the package will explicitly check
access, according

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:29.731
to the blueprint.

00:00:31.116 --> 00:00:34.636
Create the billing package and add its
charge lookup. Before

00:00:34.796 --> 00:00:38.156
reading the local ledger, the package
checks the named operation

00:00:38.236 --> 00:00:42.156
and the requested customer ID. Check the
package, then publish

00:00:42.236 --> 00:00:43.276
it to the local store.

00:00:45.096 --> 00:00:48.296
Define, review and approve the blueprint
yourself, with an

00:00:48.416 --> 00:00:52.376
agent's help if useful. Require customer
ID from the application,

00:00:52.896 --> 00:00:55.816
deny by default, and allow this lookup
only when its

00:00:55.896 --> 00:00:59.416
argument matches that context. Then lint
the blueprint.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:04.840
Start the server with a fresh local token.
The application

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:08.280
selects the configured blueprint, binds
the customer ID for this

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:12.200
request, and sends the program with that
token. The response

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:14.280
reports two charges and their total.

00:01:16.287 --> 00:01:19.887
Now run the second program. It keeps the
useful total,

00:01:20.207 --> 00:01:23.967
then tries the same lookup for another
customer. That customer

00:01:24.127 --> 00:01:27.487
ID fails the blueprint rule, so the
runtime denies the

00:01:27.567 --> 00:01:30.927
lookup and returns an error instead of a
result. The

00:01:31.247 --> 00:01:35.047
application supplies request context; the
runtime enforces the

00:01:35.087 --> 00:01:36.047
configured check.
